# Indexer schedule. Cron rather than systemd timers, because this image has no init system.
#
# Deliberately no flock wrapper: the sync takes its own lock and raises the alert when it
# finds one whose holder has shown no progress for SYNC_LOCK_STALE_SEC. Wrapping it would
# skip the run before PHP starts, so that alert could never fire and a wedged sync would go
# unreported.
#
# The sync runs as www-data, the user PHP-FPM serves the API as: the two share the logs in
# /var/log/indexer and resources/, and a log file the sync created first as root (0644) left
# the API unable to write it until the next restart's chown. It needs nothing of root's: MySQL
# over TCP with the configured user, rr-proxy over its www-data socket. logrotate stays root.
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

* * * * * www-data /usr/local/bin/indexer sync >> /var/log/indexer/sync-cron.log 2>&1
# Hourly, so a log that grows past maxsize (logrotate-indexer) is rotated within the hour; the
# daily rotations still happen once a day, by logrotate's own state.
17 * * * * root /usr/sbin/logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf >> /var/log/indexer/logrotate-cron.log 2>&1
